\subsection{DQN on Atari: Standard vs Soft}\label{dqnatari}

Here we investigated whether soft $Q$-learning (which optimizes the entropy-augmented return) performs differently from standard ``hard'' $Q$-learning on Atari.
We made a one-line change to a DQN implementation:
\begin{alignat}{2}
&y_t = r_t + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s_{t+1}, a')  &&\qquad\text{Standard}\\
&y_t = r_t + \gamma \log \sum_{a'} \exp(Q(s_{t+1}, a')/\tau)-\log\abs{\mathcal{A}}  &&\qquad\text{``Soft'': KL penalty}\\
&y_t =r_t + \gamma \log \sum_{a'} \exp(Q(s_{t+1}, a')/\tau)  &&\qquad\text{``Soft'': Entropy bonus}
\end{alignat}
The difference between the entropy bonus and KL penalty (against uniform) is simply a constant, however, this constant made a big difference in the experiments, since a positive constant added to the reward encourages longer episodes. Note that we use the same epsilon-greedy exploration in all conditions; the only difference is the backup equation used for computing $y_t$ and defining the loss function.

